A||||7||Conrad Sanville||Oxy sensor fell out||||1||
Z||000000||Conrad Sanville||10-06-2001||06:23 PM||||<BR>The oxy sensor on one of my iron ducks fell out.  It took the threads in the bung right along with it.  So now I just have a perfect round hole in the downpipe.  Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||10-06-2001||08:07 PM||||pick and pull junkyard time<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||11
Z||000002||SteveJ||10-06-2001||08:07 PM||||I had a spark plug shoot the entire porcelain part out the top. <P>It's an '84 and the rain falls on the plugs so it rusted the top of the plug off.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bazooka||10-06-2001||10:06 PM||||Conrad,   There is a good product out there called a 'Heli-Coil' which is a coiled sleeve that fits between the threads. You would have to Tap the next larger thread size into your exhaust manifold first. Its best to heat up the manifold by running the engine a few minutes then shut off and unscrew the O2 Sensor. That way the threads wont be seized like yours are presently. Also use an approved sealer on the threads so that the sensor element won't be destroyed with by-products created with the heat of the exhaust. <BR> <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>***************************<BR>Originally posted by Conrad Sanville:<BR>The oxy sensor on one of my iron ducks fell out.  It took the threads in the bung right along with it.  So now I just have a perfect round hole in the downpipe.  Any ideas?<BR>***************************<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||voyagerspe||10-06-2001||11:16 PM||||the heil coils a good idea but another way to fix it is weld the hole shut then by the oxygen sensor boss from painless wiring ($8.00 jegs) drill a hole under the old hole that you just "patched" and weld the boss on.  if you dont have a welder to use you could farm the job out for a couple of bucks(its pretty easy and shouldnt cost much)<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||Cozmo||10-07-2001||08:48 AM||||Had the same thing happen to my Duke.  It ain't pretty, but I just pushed the O2 sensor back in the hole and clamped it in place with an old bracket and a hose clamp. It was caused by excessive backpressure from a clogged cat and muffler.<P>------------------<BR>     <B>BobC</B><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||10-07-2001||01:48 PM||||With an abundunce of Duke exhaust manifolds it might just be easier to replace it. The same manifolds were used in the old Caveliers, Sunbirds, etc...I see them ALL the time in the pick a part yards. And in those engines the manifold is facing the front. 15 MM sockets and you have a new manifold!||||reg||14
Z||000007||kinboyatuwo||10-09-2001||12:19 PM||||If you weld it it will crack, trust me. The difference in expansion rates of the two different metals will cause seperation.  It might last a couple months at best!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||11
